{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task is AGENT-TF-01. I need to write a `solve(case)` function that:\n1. Takes a payload with `threshold` and `calls`\n2. Each call has: `call_id`, `loss_without_call`, `loss_with_call`, `prediction_tokens`, `parseable`\n3. Returns an object with `accepted_call_ids` and `diagnostics`\n\nEach diagnostic needs: `accepted` (bool), `call_id` (string), `normalized_gain` (number)\n\nLet me figure out the logic by analyzing the fixture data.\n\nFor each call, I need to compute a `normalized_gain` and then decide whether it's `accepted`.\n\nThe gain is `loss_without_call - loss_with_call`. The \"normalized\" part likely divides by something - probably `prediction_tokens` or `loss_without_call`.\n\nLet me think about what \"normalized gain\" could mean:\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens` - normalized by tokens\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call` - relative gain\n\nLooking at the threshold (0.05), this suggests a comparison. The call is accepted if `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold` AND `parseable == true`.\n\nWait, let me think about what normalized_gain formula makes sense with the threshold of 0.05.\n\nFor case 000:\n- call0: loss_without=1.48911432, loss_with=0.30132987, tokens=5, parseable=true\n  - raw_gain = 1.18778445\n  - normalized by tokens: 1.18778445/5 = 0.23755689\n  - normalized by loss_without: 1.18778445/1.48911432 = 0.7977...\n\n- call5: loss_without=1.98682122, loss_with=0.89260518, tokens=4, parseable=false\n  - This one is not parseable, so likely rejected regardless\n\nThe threshold of 0.05 seems quite low. Let me think...\n\nMaybe normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call (relative improvement)?\n\nFor all parseable=true calls, the gains would be quite large (much larger than 0.05), so they'd all be accepted if parseable.\n\nMaybe the condition for acceptance is:\n1. `parseable == true`\n2. `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nWhere normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\n\nFor case 000:\n- call3: loss_without=1.29764071, loss_with=0.978432, tokens=5, parseable=true\n  - gain = 1.29764071 - 0.978432 = 0.31920871\n  - normalized = 0.31920871 / 1.29764071 = 0.2460...\n  - accepted? gain \u003e threshold and parseable → yes\n\nFor case 002, call3:\n- loss_without=1.29764071, loss_with=0.978432, parseable=true\n- gain = 0.319208, normalized = 0.2460... → accepted\n\nFor case 002, call4:\n- loss_without=1.91348117, loss_with=0.88407266, parseable=true\n- gain = 1.02940851, normalized = 0.5380... → accepted\n\nAll parseable=true calls in the fixtures seem to have large gains that would exceed 0.05.\n\nLooking at call5 in case 000: parseable=false, so rejected regardless of gain.\n\nIn case 001:\n- call1: parseable=false → rejected\n- call3: parseable=false → rejected\n- call5: parseable=false → rejected\n\nSo accepted calls in case 001 would be call0, call2, call4 (parseable=true).\n\nLet me check if the normalized gain formula could be different. Maybe it's:\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / (loss_without_call * prediction_tokens)\n\nFor call0 in case 000: (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / (1.48911432 * 5) = 1.18778445 / 7.4455716 = 0.1596...\n\nStill \u003e 0.05.\n\nOr maybe it's just the raw loss difference divided by number of tokens:\n(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens\n\nFor the most \"marginal\" cases... Looking at case 002 call3:\n- gain = 1.29764071 - 0.978432 = 0.319208\n- /5 = 0.0638... \u003e 0.05\n\nCase 002 call4:\n- gain = 1.91348117 - 0.88407266 = 1.02940851\n- /6 = 0.1715... \u003e 0.05\n\nHmm, none seem close to the threshold in the given fixtures.\n\nActually, I think normalized_gain might just be (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call, a relative improvement metric. And the acceptance criterion is parseable=true AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold.\n\nBut wait - could there be cases where normalized_gain \u003c threshold for parseable=true calls? Let me check case 002 call3:\n- loss_without=1.29764071, loss_with=0.978432\n- gain = 0.31920871\n- relative = 0.31920871/1.29764071 = 0.2460... → accepted\n\nNo call in the fixtures seems to be close to the threshold boundary.\n\nLet me think differently. Maybe normalized_gain is just the difference divided by something more standard. The \"normalized\" could mean normalized to [0,1] range by dividing by loss_without_call.\n\nThe key logic seems to be:\n1. normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\n2. accepted = parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n\nLet me verify with the simplest interpretation. Given the threshold is 0.05 and all gains in the fixtures are large, any parseable=true call would be accepted.\n\nActually, I think the task might be simpler than I'm making it. Let me re-read.\n\nThe task is about \"tool calls\" (hence AGENT-TF-01, TF = \"tool-call filtering\"?). The idea is:\n- You have baseline loss and loss with call\n- You compute how much the call helped\n- If it helped enough (normalized gain \u003e= threshold) and is parseable, accept it\n\nThe most natural normalized gain formula would be:\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\n\nThis measures relative improvement. A value of 0 means no improvement, 1 means perfect improvement.\n\nFor acceptance:\n- parseable must be true\n- normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n\nLet me code this up.\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        if loss_without == 0:\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\n        else:\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without\n        \n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain \u003e= threshold)\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but I'm not using `prediction_tokens` at all in this formula. That seems odd.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe:\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens\n\nThis would be \"per-token gain\" normalization.\n\nFor case 000, call3: (1.76582941 - 0.57732788) / 3 = 1.18850153/3 = 0.39616717 \u003e 0.05 → accepted if parseable\nFor case 000, call5: (1.98682122 - 0.89260518) / 4 = 1.09421604/4 = 0.27355401, but parseable=false → rejected\n\nThis formula uses prediction_tokens, which makes more sense.\n\nActually, thinking about it more carefully - in the context of \"tool call filtering\" for language models:\n- `loss_without_call` is the model's loss when predicting the output without using the tool call\n- `loss_with_call` is the model's loss when predicting the output with the tool call's result\n- `prediction_tokens` is the number of tokens being predicted\n\nThe normalized gain per token would be:\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens\n\nThis makes sense as a \"information gain per prediction token\" metric.\n\nLet me check case 002:\n- call3: (1.29764071 - 0.978432) / 5 = 0.31920871/5 = 0.06384174 \u003e 0.05 → accepted (parseable=true)\n- call4: (1.91348117 - 0.88407266) / 6 = 1.02940851/6 = 0.17156808 \u003e 0.05 → accepted (parseable=true)\n\nHmm, both would be accepted. But what about case 002 call5:\n- (1.2515065 - 0.60014613) / 3 = 0.65136037/3 = 0.21712012 \u003e 0.05 → accepted\n\nAll parseable=true calls would still be accepted.\n\nWithout knowing the expected outputs, I need to pick the most reasonable formula. Given the context (tool call filtering) and the fact that prediction_tokens is provided, I'll use:\n\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens\n\nAnd acceptance = parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The threshold is 0.05 which is quite low. If we use relative gain (divided by loss_without_call), all large gains would be way above 0.05. If we use per-token gain, same thing. \n\nMaybe the threshold is for a different formula. Let me think about what makes the threshold meaningful with value 0.05...\n\nIn NLP/ML contexts with cross-entropy loss, a per-token gain of 0.05 nats/bits would be a meaningful threshold. The formula (loss_without - loss_with) / prediction_tokens gives the average per-token improvement in cross-entropy loss.\n\nI'll go with that formula. Let me write the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        prediction_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        if prediction_tokens \u003e 0:\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / prediction_tokens\n        else:\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\n        \n        accepted = bool(parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold)\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nThis seems like the right approach. The output format matches the schema requirement.\n\nNow I need to format this as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        prediction_tokens = call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n\\n        if prediction_tokens \u003e 0:\\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / prediction_tokens\\n        else:\\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\\n\\n        accepted = bool(parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold)\\n\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain,\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted\\n        })\\n\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01DCGVH5Uz74rqFAnUjPKX27","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2111},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2111,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":3143,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2872},"service_tier":"standard"}}